export default {"id":"bassstation-level-11","number":11,"title":"Analogue Effects: Distortion & Oscillator Filter Modulation","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Analyze the physical circuit placement and acoustic properties of the discrete analogue Distortion stage. - Characterize the post-filter, pre-VCA signal topology, explaining why distortion character remains constant across decaying amplit","modules":[{"id":"bassstation-m-11-1","code":"11","title":"Analogue Effects: Distortion & Oscillator Filter Modulation","blurb":"- Analyze the physical circuit placement and acoustic properties of the discrete analogue Distortion stage. - Characterize the post-filter, pre-VCA signal topology, explaining why distortion character remains constant across decaying amplit","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"bassstation-l-o-11","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 11.1 — Analogue Effects Architecture and Signal Flow","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Section Learning Outcomes","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Analyze the physical circuit placement and acoustic properties of the discrete analogue <strong>Distortion</strong> stage.","Characterize the post-filter, pre-VCA signal topology, explaining why distortion character remains constant across decaying amplitude envelopes.","Master direct audio-rate frequency modulation of the filter cutoff via <strong>Oscillator Filter Modulation (Osc Filter Mod)</strong>.","Contrast and combine pre-filter <strong>Overdrive</strong> with post-filter <strong>Distortion</strong> for multi-stage analog saturation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-11-1-1","title":"11.1.1: Post-Filter Analogue Distortion","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Bass Station II features a dedicated discrete analogue Distortion circuit positioned directly after the filter section and before the final Voltage Controlled Amplifier (VCA):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                      ANALOGUE EFFECTS SIGNAL CHAIN\n   [Analogue Mixer] ──► [Pre-Filter Overdrive] ──► [Analogue Filter]\n                                                          │\n                                                          ▼\n                                              [POST-FILTER DISTORTION]\n                                                          │\n                                                          ▼\n                                                  [Discrete VCA]\n                                                          │\n                                                          ▼\n                                                 [Line Out / Phones]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Pre-VCA Topological Significance</strong>:","In external guitar distortion pedals or post-VCA effects, as a note decays in volume, the signal level drops below the distortion clipping threshold, causing the sound to clean up and lose harmonic bite as it fades.","On the Bass Station II, <strong>Distortion is positioned BEFORE the VCA</strong>.","<em>Acoustic Result</em>: The harmonic grit, fuzz, and clipping saturation remain <strong>100% constant in timbral character</strong> throughout the entire decay and release phases of the amplitude envelope, regardless of how quiet the note becomes.","<strong>Harmonic Characteristics</strong>:","The Distortion potentiometer scales from <code>0</code> (clean bypass) to <code>127</code> (hard asymmetrical clipping).","Generates aggressive odd and even distortion harmonics, compressing dynamic peaks and turning smooth saw/pulse waves into raw, crushing industrial tones."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load Init patch <code>64</code>. Set Amp Envelope to a slow decay (Decay 50%, Sustain 0, Release 50%).","Play a note with Distortion at 0; listen to the smooth, clean saw fade to silence.","Turn <strong>Distortion</strong> to maximum (127). Strike the note again.","Listen carefully as the sound fades away; observe that the hard, buzzing distortion texture remains completely saturated and gritty all the way into final silence."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Where is the Distortion circuit located relative to the Filter and the VCA?","[ ] Why does the distortion character not clean up as a note decays in volume?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-11-1-2","title":"11.1.2: Oscillator Filter Modulation (Osc Filter Mod)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Osc Filter Mod</strong> is an audio-rate analog Frequency Modulation (FM) routing that takes the raw output waveform of <strong>Oscillator 2</strong> and feeds it directly into the control voltage input of the <strong>Filter Cutoff frequency</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                 OSCILLATOR FILTER MODULATION (FM) TOPOLOGY\n   [Oscillator 2 Core] (Waveform, Coarse/Fine Pitch, Mod Env)\n           │\n           ▼ (Audio-Rate Modulation Signal: 20 Hz to &gt; 10 kHz)\n   [OSC FILTER MOD Pot (0–127)]\n           │\n           ▼\n   [Filter Cutoff Frequency CV Input] ◄── Sweeps Cutoff at Audio Speed!</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Audio-Rate Filter Sweeping</strong>:","Unlike an LFO (which sweeps cutoff at sub-audio speeds up to ~190 Hz), Oscillator 2 sweeps the filter cutoff at the exact fundamental frequency of the note being played (e.g., hundreds or thousands of times per second).","Produces complex sideband generation, aggressive metallic formants, robotic growls, and bell-like timbres.","<strong>Parameter Interactions with Oscillator 2</strong>:","The timbre of Osc Filter Mod is dictated entirely by the settings of <strong>Oscillator 2</strong>:","<em>Waveform</em>: Sine produces pure, smooth 2-operator FM tones; Sawtooth/Pulse produces complex, abrasive harmonic dirt.","<em>Pitch Tuning</em>: Adjusting Osc 2 <strong>Coarse</strong> and <strong>Fine</strong> tuning drastically shifts the FM ratio and sideband spacing.","<em>Pitch Modulation</em>: Sweeping Osc 2 pitch via the Pitch Wheel or Modulation Envelope while Osc Filter Mod is raised creates screaming, morphing FM laser sweeps."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On an Init patch, set <strong>Osc 1</strong> to 100 in the mixer, and <strong>Osc 2</strong> to 0 in the mixer.","Set Osc 2 to <strong>Sine wave</strong>, Range <strong>8'</strong>.","Turn <strong>Filter Frequency</strong> to 50% and Resonance to 40%.","Turn <strong>Osc Filter Mod</strong> from 0 up to 80%.","Slowly rotate the <strong>Coarse</strong> and <strong>Fine</strong> tuning controls of Osc 2; hear the rich, metallic FM sidebands shift dynamically."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Which oscillator provides the modulation source for the Osc Filter Mod parameter?","[ ] What is the sonic difference between modulating the filter with an LFO versus Osc Filter Mod?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-11-1-3","title":"11.1.3: Pre-Filter Overdrive vs. Post-Filter Distortion","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Bass Station II provides two distinct analog saturation stages:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    OVERDRIVE VS. DISTORTION COMPARISON                     │\n├───────────────┬───────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Feature       │ Pre-Filter Overdrive      │ Post-Filter Distortion         │\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Location      │ BEFORE the Filter         │ AFTER the Filter (Pre-VCA)     │\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Interaction   │ Adds harmonics for filter  │ Distorts the filtered result;  │\n│ with Filter   │ cutoff &amp; resonance to cut │ clips high resonance peaks     │\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Acoustic Tone │ Warm, creamy, thick,      │ Aggressive, gritty, biting,    │\n│               │ analogue drive saturation │ crushing fuzz / hard clip      │\n└───────────────┴───────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Multi-Stage Saturation Strategy</strong>:","Combining mild pre-filter Overdrive (value ~40–60) with moderate post-filter Distortion (value ~30–50) yields a rich, cohesive, three-dimensional analog tone with controlled resonance and dense harmonic sustain."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Program a low resonant bass (Classic LP 24, Cutoff 40%, Resonance 70%).","Compare A/B:","<em>Test A (Overdrive Only)</em>: Overdrive at 100, Distortion at 0. (Warm, rounded saturation).","<em>Test B (Distortion Only)</em>: Overdrive at 0, Distortion at 100. (Sharp, aggressive fuzz).","<em>Test C (Combined)</em>: Overdrive at 50, Distortion at 50. (Dense, balanced saturation)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
